{{!--
  Header toolbar version tag (see GH issue #7). Reuses the `.ids-tag` block
  from tag/tag.css (`Solid` + `Selected` in the design) rather than the
  Select Tag component — Select Tag pulls in list/scrollable-panel/search-field
  CSS for a dropdown menu this bundle already renders itself below.

  GH-101: gated on `page.componentVersion` (always present for a real page),
  never on `page.versions` — Antora's page-composer only computes
  `page.versions` when `component.versions.length > 1`
  (@antora/page-composer build-ui-model.js); a single-version component (e.g.
  `main` alone, no `stable` tag yet) gets `page.versions === undefined`, and
  gating the whole partial on it — the previous behaviour — meant NEITHER
  branch below ever rendered, not even this one. `page.componentVersion` has
  no such gap, so it's the one truth source both branches key off.

  Green (`header-toolbar__version--latest`) applies to the dropdown toggle
  when the page's version IS the component's latest — `(eq page.componentVersion
  page.component.latest)`, both always populated by Antora. The old
  `page.componentVersion.latest` this replaced was dead: Antora never sets a
  `latest` boolean ON a version descriptor, only `component.latest` as an
  object reference to it (@antora/content-classifier content-catalog.js), so
  that condition was never true for any build.

  The single-version badge below deliberately does NOT reuse that check.
  Antora's fallback — a component with only prerelease versions still gets
  `component.latest` pointed at the sole one (there's no "no latest" state) —
  means `eq page.componentVersion page.component.latest` is trivially true
  whenever there's only one version, prerelease or not. Applying `--latest`
  here would paint a prerelease-only build green, i.e. visually claim
  "stable" for a preview build. Giving Stable vs Prerelease their own explicit
  treatment is GH-103's job; until then this stays the DS's plain read-only
  solid tag.

  `ids-tag--read-only` (DS modifier, tag/tag.css) marks the badge as
  non-interactive — it isn't a button and does nothing on click, so it must
  not pick up `.ids-tag`'s generic hover cursor/colour shift, which would
  otherwise imply an affordance that isn't there. `aria-label` adds the
  "Version" context the bare displayVersion text doesn't carry on its own.
  The label is always the real `displayVersion` Antora computed; nothing
  here synthesises text.
--}}
{{#if page.componentVersion}}
<div class="page-versions">
  {{#if page.versions.[1]}}
  <button type="button" class="ids-tag ids-tag--solid ids-tag--selected header-toolbar__version{{#if (eq page.componentVersion page.component.latest)}} header-toolbar__version--latest{{/if}} version-menu-toggle" title="Show other versions of page">
    <span class="ids-tag__label">{{page.componentVersion.displayVersion}}</span>
    <span class="ids-tag__icon">{{icon "arrows" "chevron-down-outlined"}}</span>
  </button>
  <div class="version-menu">
    {{#each page.versions}}
    <a class="version
      {{~#if (eq ./version @root.page.version)}} is-current{{/if~}}
      {{~#if ./missing}} is-missing{{/if}}" href="{{{relativize ./url}}}">{{./displayVersion}}</a>
    {{/each}}
  </div>
  {{else}}
  <span class="ids-tag ids-tag--solid ids-tag--selected ids-tag--read-only header-toolbar__version" aria-label="Version {{page.componentVersion.displayVersion}}">
    <span class="ids-tag__label">{{page.componentVersion.displayVersion}}</span>
  </span>
  {{/if}}
</div>
{{/if}}
